Barnaby was not built for adventure. He was a plump orange tabby who spent most of his days napping in sunbeams, judging the birds from the windowsill, and demanding breakfast at exactly 6:00 a.m.

But one rainy Tuesday, a strange black cat appeared on the garden wall.

It had one white paw and eyes like green glass. It sat perfectly still, staring at Barnaby through the kitchen window.

Barnaby pressed his nose to the glass.

The black cat tilted its head, then leaped down and disappeared into the hedge.

Something about that look—calm, daring, almost mocking—made Barnaby’s tail twitch. He padded to the back door and meowed. His owner, Lily, opened it without looking up from her book.

“Fine,” she said. “But don’t get muddy.”

Barnaby stepped into the rain.

The garden smelled of wet earth and crushed mint. He followed the black cat’s trail past the tomato plants, under the rose bushes, and through a gap in the fence he had never noticed before. On the other side was a narrow path lined with weeds and old fence posts.

He had never been beyond the garden before.

The world was bigger than he expected.

He trotted down the path, his paws sinking into soft mud. The rain tapped against his fur, and the trees whispered above him. After a while, he heard a sound: a low, mournful cry.

He stopped.

At the edge of a ditch, a small brown kitten was trapped beneath a fallen branch. It was soaked and shivering, mewing weakly.

Barnaby looked around. The black cat was nowhere to be seen.

He approached cautiously. The kitten hissed, then coughed.

Barnaby crouched beside the branch. It was too heavy to lift with his paws, but he noticed a loose stick nearby. He nudged it under the branch with his nose, pushing and pushing until the branch shifted just enough.

The kitten scrambled free.

For a moment, the two cats stared at each other.

Then the kitten meekly followed him.

They walked together down the path, the kitten staying close to Barnaby’s legs. When they reached the gap in the fence, the black cat was waiting.

It looked at the kitten, then at Barnaby.

“Not bad,” it said, in a voice like a purr.

Barnaby blinked. “You can talk?”

The black cat smiled. “Only to cats who’ve earned it.”

Barnaby looked down at the kitten, who was now rubbing against his leg. He felt something warm in his chest that had nothing to do with sunbeams.

He had always thought adventure was something that happened to other cats.

Now he knew better.

He led the kitten home through the rain, proud and muddy and brave, already wondering what tomorrow would bring.
